1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A direct-address table stores the key itself at index k. One array access, no search at all.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
It works only when the universe of keys is small enough to be an array index.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
So we keep m slots and compute a slot from the key: h maps a big key to a small number.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Two keys can land on one slot. That is a collision, and it is not a mistake in the function.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Division method: h of k is k modulo m. Fast, and good when m is prime.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Take m a power of two and the function reads only the low bits of the key. A prime m mixes all of them.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Multiplication method: multiply by a constant, keep the fractional part, scale to m.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
What a hash function owes you: it must be fast, and it must spread any key set you actually meet.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Fix one published hash function and an adversary can choose keys that all collide.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Universal hashing picks the function at random from a family, after the adversary has committed.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The guarantee: for any two distinct keys, at most one in m functions of the family makes them collide.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
So the expected number of keys in a slot is n over m, whatever the key set. That is the load factor.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Chaining puts a linked list at every slot. A colliding key is appended to the list.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The load factor alpha is n over m, the average number of keys per slot. With chaining it can exceed 1.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Under uniform hashing an unsuccessful search scans 1 plus alpha nodes; a successful one scans 1 plus alpha over 2.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
GATE CSE 2014 Set 1: nine keys into nine slots by chaining gives maximum 3, minimum 0, average 1.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Open addressing stores the keys themselves in the m slots. No lists, no pointers, no extra memory.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
A collision starts a probe sequence: a fixed walk through the table until a free slot is found.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Linear probing with h of x equals x plus 7 modulo 11: each collision moves one slot right, wrapping at the end.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The result: slots 0, 1, 7, 8, 9, 10 hold keys, and 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 are empty.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Every key that hashes into a run walks to the far end of it, and lengthens the run by one.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Knuth's result: an unsuccessful search under linear probing averages half of 1 plus 1 over the square of 1 minus alpha.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The same four load factors, measured on the page: linear probing is the worst of the three, and the gap widens.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
A successful search is cheaper, because it can stop at its own key. Linear probing: half of 1 plus 1 over 1 minus alpha.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Quadratic probing adds a square: the i-th probe is the home slot plus c1 i plus c2 i squared, modulo m.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
With m a power of two the walk can miss half the table. With m prime and alpha at most one half, it is guaranteed to find a slot.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Double hashing steps by a second hash: probe i is h1 plus i times h2, modulo m.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Two traces: key 90 gives probe 1 equal to 13 in 2020, and key 24 lands in slot 10 in 2025 Set 1.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Delete a key by marking its slot empty, and every key that walked past it becomes unreachable.

30
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,500
The fix is a tombstone: mark the slot deleted, which search treats as occupied and insert treats as free.

31
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:48,000
Tombstones accumulate: a table of tombstones has no free slot yet no keys, so the table must be rebuilt.

32
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Chaining has no such problem: given the pointer, unlinking is O of 1. That is why GATE CSE 1996 chose deletion as its advantage.

33
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Four formulas, one assumption. Every one of them is an average under uniform hashing.

34
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
At alpha equal to 0.5 the three schemes are close; at 0.9 they are not. Linear probing pays 50 probes against 10.

35
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Successful search is cheaper than unsuccessful in every scheme. The gap is not a constant.

36
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:30,000
So hashing is O of 1 on average, and Theta of n in the worst case. Both statements are true, of different things.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
First: which scheme? Chaining has lists; linear, quadratic and double are all open addressing.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Second: is the answer a count or an estimate? Given keys and an order, trace. Given only n, m and alpha, use a formula.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Third: does the stem name a probe number? Then substitute i, and remember probe 0 is the first probe.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Fourth: is the question about a search that succeeds, or one that fails? The formulas differ, and so do the traces.

